The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
while (1) {
	list = llist_del_all(&new_wb->switch_wbs_ctxs);
	/* Nothing to do? */
	if (!list)
		break;
```

Reason:
The loop repeatedly drains `wb->switch_wbs_ctxs` and processes items. If a new item is added (and work re-queued) while the loop is running, that item will also be processed in the same loop iteration. After the loop exits (list empty), the work may still be pending because the re-queue occurred. When the pending work later runs, the list is already empty, so no reference to `wb` is held by any isw item. The `wb_get()` at the start of the work function then accesses a freed `wb`, causing a use-after-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
list = llist_del_all(&new_wb->switch_wbs_ctxs);
/*
 * Nothing to do? That would be a problem as references held by isw
 * items protect wb from freeing...
 */
if (WARN_ON_ONCE(!list))
	return;

/*
 * Grab our reference to wb so that it cannot get freed under us
 * after we process all the isw items.
 */
wb_get(new_wb);
```

Reason:
The fix removes the `while` loop, so the work function drains the entire list exactly once when it runs. This guarantees that the work is only queued when the first item is added to `switch_wbs_ctxs`, and the list will never be empty when the work runs (because the work is not re-queued while still processing). Thus `wb_get()` is always called with at least one isw item holding a reference to `wb`, preventing the use-after-free. The `WARN_ON_ONCE` catches any unexpected empty-list scenario.

